---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "THE CENTRAL CHOICE — Two Entirely Different Paradigms" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Taxes Payable Method" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Disclosure Still Required Under the Taxes Payable Method" },
        { id: "s6", label: "The Future Income Taxes Method" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Recognition of Deferred Tax Assets — a \"More Likely Than Not\" Threshold" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Why This Choice Exists" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — the Taxes Payable Method" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example — the Future Income Taxes Method for the Same Facts" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Relationship to IAS 12" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A genuine choice between two fundamentally different income tax accounting models</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3465 governs the ACCOUNTING for INCOME taxes — AND, GENUINELY distinctively, OFFERS an ENTERPRISE a CHOICE between TWO entirely DIFFERENT measurement PARADIGMS: the TAXES payable METHOD (RECOGNIZING no DEFERRED tax WHATSOEVER) or the FUTURE income TAXES method (a COMPREHENSIVE, balance-SHEET deferred TAX approach). THIS choice HAS no EQUIVALENT anywhere ELSE in THIS entire POSTER library (Section 3).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Income taxes payable and, where elected, deferred tax</div><div class="principle">This Section applies TO the ACCOUNTING for CURRENT and (WHERE elected) DEFERRED income TAXES arising FROM an ENTERPRISE'S own OPERATIONS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>THE CENTRAL CHOICE — Two Entirely Different Paradigms</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Not a choice within a shared framework — a choice OF framework</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Method</span><span>Description</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Taxes Payable Method</span><span>RECOGNIZES tax EXPENSE simply EQUAL to the AMOUNT of INCOME taxes PAYABLE (or REFUNDABLE) for the CURRENT period, based ON the enterprise'S own TAX return — <strong>NO</strong> deferred TAX assets OR liabilities are RECOGNIZED at ALL</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Future Income Taxes Method</span><span>A COMPREHENSIVE, balance-SHEET, temporary-DIFFERENCE-based APPROACH — BROADLY similar TO ASC 740/IAS 12'S own DEFERRED tax MODEL, recognizing DEFERRED tax ASSETS and LIABILITIES for TEMPORARY differences BETWEEN accounting AND tax bases</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Taxes Payable Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Radically simpler — tax expense equals the tax return liability</div><div class="text-supporting">UNDER the TAXES payable METHOD, INCOME tax EXPENSE for THE period simply EQUALS the AMOUNT of INCOME taxes PAYABLE (or REFUNDABLE), as DETERMINED by the ENTERPRISE'S own TAX return CALCULATION — TEMPORARY differences BETWEEN the ACCOUNTING carrying AMOUNTS of ASSETS and LIABILITIES and THEIR respective TAX bases are SIMPLY ignored, WITH no DEFERRED tax ASSET or LIABILITY ever RECOGNIZED.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Disclosure Still Required Under the Taxes Payable Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Visibility into unrecognized temporary differences, even without balance sheet recognition</div><div class="text-supporting">EVEN WHERE the TAXES payable METHOD is ELECTED, an ENTERPRISE STILL discloses the NATURE and, WHERE practicable, the AMOUNT of SIGNIFICANT temporary DIFFERENCES that ARE NOT reflected WITHIN the ACCOUNTS — GIVING readers SOME visibility INTO the potential FUTURE tax CONSEQUENCES of THESE unrecognized DIFFERENCES, DESPITE the SIMPLER measurement APPROACH chosen.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Future Income Taxes Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A full deferred tax balance sheet approach</div><div class="text-supporting">UNDER the FUTURE income TAXES method, an ENTERPRISE recognizes DEFERRED tax ASSETS and LIABILITIES for ALL temporary DIFFERENCES between the ACCOUNTING carrying AMOUNTS of ASSETS and LIABILITIES and THEIR respective TAX bases — MEASURED using ENACTED, or SUBSTANTIVELY enacted, TAX rates EXPECTED to APPLY when the TEMPORARY differences REVERSE, BROADLY consistent WITH the GENERAL deferred-TAX approach ALSO used INTERNATIONALLY.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Recognition of Deferred Tax Assets — a "More Likely Than Not" Threshold</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A recognition threshold styled closer to US GAAP than IAS 12</div><div class="text-supporting">UNDER the FUTURE income TAXES method, a DEFERRED tax ASSET is RECOGNIZED only TO the EXTENT it IS "MORE likely THAN not" to BE realized — a THRESHOLD conceptually STYLED closer TO the US GAAP ASC 740 APPROACH than to IAS 12'S OWN differently-PHRASED recognition CRITERIA, THOUGH broadly SIMILAR in SUBSTANCE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Why This Choice Exists</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Deferred tax accounting is expensive, and often low-value, for many private enterprises</div><div class="text-supporting">DEFERRED tax ACCOUNTING is GENUINELY complex and COSTLY to APPLY — REQUIRING ongoing TRACKING of TEMPORARY differences, TAX rate FORECASTING, and VALUATION allowance ASSESSMENTS. FOR MANY private ENTERPRISES, PARTICULARLY smaller ONES with RELATIVELY simple TAX positions, the INCREMENTAL informational VALUE of a FULL deferred TAX balance SHEET is GENUINELY limited RELATIVE to its COST — ASPE'S standard-SETTERS therefore PRESERVED the SIMPLER taxes PAYABLE method AS a GENUINE, unrestricted ALTERNATIVE, REFLECTING the SAME cost-BENEFIT philosophy UNDERLYING virtually EVERY other OPTIONAL feature we'VE documented THROUGHOUT this ENTIRE ASPE poster SERIES.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — the Taxes Payable Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Simply the amount owed to the tax authority</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A small private company's tax expense</div><div class="wk-body">A small manufacturing company, electing the taxes payable method, calculates $180,000 of income tax payable for the current year based on its tax return, despite having $95,000 of unrecognized temporary differences (primarily accelerated tax depreciation) that would generate a deferred tax liability under the alternative method.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY recognizes INCOME tax EXPENSE of EXACTLY <strong>$180,000</strong> — MATCHING its TAX return LIABILITY precisely, WITH <strong>NO</strong> deferred TAX liability RECOGNIZED for the $95,000 of TEMPORARY differences, THOUGH THEIR nature and AMOUNT are DISCLOSED in the NOTES.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — the Future Income Taxes Method for the Same Facts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A materially different reported result for identical underlying economics</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: The same company, electing the alternative method</div><div class="wk-body">The same company instead elects the future income taxes method, with the $95,000 of temporary differences (net) requiring recognition of a deferred tax liability, using a substantively enacted 25% tax rate.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY recognizes a $95,000 × 25% = $23,750 DEFERRED tax LIABILITY, WITH total INCOME tax EXPENSE of $180,000 (CURRENT) + $23,750 (DEFERRED) = <strong>$203,750</strong> — a MATERIALLY DIFFERENT reported RESULT than the $180,000 the TAXES payable METHOD produced, FOR the EXACT same UNDERLYING economic FACTS.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3465 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policy elected (Taxes Payable Method or Future Income Taxes Method)</span><span>Under the Taxes Payable Method: nature and amount of significant unrecognized temporary differences</span><span>Under the Future Income Taxes Method: components of deferred tax assets and liabilities, and any valuation allowance</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Which method genuinely best serves the enterprise's users, weighing cost against informational value</span><span>Whether a deferred tax asset is more likely than not to be realized, under the Future Income Taxes Method</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Substantively enacted tax rates expected to apply when temporary differences reverse</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 12</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 3465 vs IAS 12</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3465</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 12</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Deferred tax opt-out</span><span>AVAILABLE — Taxes Payable Method</span><span>NOT available — deferred tax mandatory</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Future Income Taxes Method mechanics, where elected</span><span>Broadly converged</span><span>Broadly converged</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">ARGUABLY the SINGLE most FUNDAMENTAL divergence THIS entire POSTER library HAS documented — NOT a difference IN how a SHARED concept IS applied, but a GENUINE choice BETWEEN two ENTIRELY different MEASUREMENT paradigms FOR the SAME underlying ECONOMIC phenomenon.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every income tax event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Current tax expense</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Recognized under both methods</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Deferred tax asset/liability</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>ONLY recognized under the Future Income Taxes Method</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1505</span><span>Disclosure of Accounting Policies — requires disclosure of which income tax method has been elected</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Taxes Payable Method</div><div class="glossary-def">An income tax accounting policy recognizing tax expense simply equal to the current period's tax return liability, with no deferred tax recognized.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Future Income Taxes Method</div><div class="glossary-def">A comprehensive, balance-sheet deferred tax accounting policy recognizing deferred tax assets and liabilities for temporary differences between accounting and tax bases.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3465 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
